---

## 1.4 The six rules — the checklist for mod #151

Print this. Six months from now, when someone says "can we add X," you run these six checks
and the answer falls out.

**R1 — RATE BAND.** Every track must land `0.005 ≤ R ≤ 0.018`, where `R = (P−1.0)/(H×T)` and
`P = 1.00` is Sharp-V netherite at 18.0 DPS. If you can't estimate P and H, you can't add
the mod yet.

**R2 — CEILING.** No obtainable weapon may exceed **13 base damage at 1.0 attack speed**
(≈16.25 DPS with Sharpness V) unless it sits behind ≥30 hours of boss content. This is the
Cataclysm ceiling and it is deliberately below an enchanted netherite sword's raw DPS.
Anything above it is claiming to be stronger than a mod that takes 40 hours to finish.

**R3 — FLOOR.** No track may deliver `R < 0.005`. A track under the floor gets **shortened**
(cut required steps) or **buffed** (raise the payout). It does not get left alone because
"nothing is overpowered."

**R4 — AXIS MATCH.** Every track is advertised in Section 4 as a fair swap for ≥1 sibling.
It must pay **≥60% of its ΔP in the same currency mix** as that sibling. Currencies:
`damage · survivability · mobility · resource economy · building/aesthetic · utility`.
Two tracks equal in magnitude but paying in different currencies still read as unfair.

**R5 — GIFTING DIVISOR.** Six players share one world and will trade gear. If a track's
reward is **transferable, permanent, and replicable**, its real rate is
`R × min(6, beneficiaries)`. It must land in the band **after** that multiplication.
If it can't, the reward must be made **bound**, **consumable**, or **cheaply replicable by
anyone** (in which case the track's value is the recipe, not the item).

**R6 — TEDIUM ISOLATION.** Any track with `T ≥ 1.25` may **not** be a prerequisite for
another track, and must sit in the **upper half of the band** (`R ≥ 0.011`). Grind you chose
is a hobby. Grind you were forced into is a tax. This rule is the one that kills the
Create-as-armor-gate mistake before it happens.

**R7 (procedural) — NO ROW, NO ENTRY.** A mod does not go in the pack until it has a filled
row in the Section 1.3 table with a named person who measured H. "It looks fine" is how you
get a 30-damage sword.

**R8 — VARIANCE CAP.** *(added by completeness review — R1–R7 measure only the mean, and the
mean is not what your friends compare.)* A track's payout **spread** must stay within **1.5×**
between an unlucky and a lucky run of identical length. Two players who both did 20 hours of
Apotheosis can differ by **2–3×** on affix rolls alone — that is your stated failure mode
occurring *inside a single mod*, where no amount of cross-track parity tuning can reach it.
Fix by **narrowing ranges, not just lowering ceilings** (`violent` +2.0→+3.5 is a better fix
than +2.0→+8.0 with a lower cap), and by converting chance-based procs to deterministic ones —
the Simply Swords: Overhaul template in §2.2. **A high-variance track is strictly worse than a
deterministic track at the same mean**, because someone always draws the short straw and it
reads as unfairness even though the design was symmetric.

**R9 — AXIS EXEMPTION MUST BE DECLARED, NOT DISCOVERED.** Any track that cannot pay in combat
currency at all (Create, and per Doc 00 §8.1 likely **Cobblemon** and **Jurassic Reborn**) is
**exempt from R1/R2/R3/R5** — but the exemption is only safe if it is **written in the track
menu before anyone picks it.** An undeclared exemption is not a balance decision, it is a player
finding out at hour 60 that their track was never going to make them stronger. **Exempt loudly
or not at all.**

> **Rule count note:** the heading says six; there are nine. R8 and R9 were added by review.

---

## 1.5 The second-order effects (the owner didn't raise these; they matter)

### 1.5.1 Reward-axis mismatch

Equal-but-different still reads as unfair. Concrete mismatched pairs in this pack:

| Pair | Magnitude | Axis problem |
|---|---|---|
| **Artifacts/Relics** vs **Cataclysm** | Similar R | Trinkets pay in *mobility + utility*; Cataclysm pays in *raw damage + AoE*. The trinket player watches the Forge player one-shot things and feels weak, despite equal R. |
| **Create** vs **any combat track** | Wildly unequal | Create pays in *resource economy + building*. It has almost no combat currency at all. It should never be measured on the same axis, and it should never gate combat gear (see R6). |
| **Ars Nouveau** vs **Iron's Spellbooks** | Iron's ~2x | Both pay in *damage + utility* — same axis, so the gap is directly felt. This is the **worst** kind of mismatch: same currency, different amount. Highest priority to fix. |
| **YUNG's exploration** vs **Voidscape** | YUNG's lower | Both pay in *loot*, but YUNG's is a wide shallow stream and Voidscape is a single deep payout. Perceptually the Voidscape player "got a thing" and the YUNG's player "got some stuff." Fix by giving YUNG's exploration a *named capstone* item, not by raising its total loot. |

**Design rule that falls out:** every track needs **one nameable capstone reward**. Humans
compare trophies, not spreadsheets. A track with diffuse rewards will always feel worse than
an equal track with one trophy.

### 1.5.2 Shared world / loot gifting — this is the sleeper problem

All six share one world. If one player finishes Cataclysm, does the group get six Void Forges?

| Reward type | Giftable? | Effective cost | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| Cataclysm boss weapons (Infernal/Void Forge) | **Yes**, and bosses are **re-fightable** | H/1 per copy | ✅ Fine — each copy costs another boss kill |
| Simply Swords uniques | **Yes**, from loot chests | H/6 | 🔴 One lucky chest arms the group |
| Apotheosis affix gear | **Yes**, freely | H/6 | 🔴 One farmer arms the group |
| Iron's Spellbooks armor sets | **Yes**, craftable | H/6, then **replicable indefinitely** | 🔴 Worst case — the *recipe* is the reward |
| Ars Nouveau spell knowledge | **No** — per-player glyph unlocks | H/1 | ✅ Structurally correct |
| Artifacts trinkets | **Yes**, but loot-random and slot-limited | H/2–3 | 🟡 Acceptable |
| Create automation output | **Yes**, and it's a *factory* | H/6 and **falling over time** | 🔴 by rate, ✅ by intent — this is what Create *is*. Exempt it from R5 and never gate combat behind it. |

**Two actions fall out of this table:**
1. **Install Lootr** (per-player structure chests). It removes the first-to-the-chest race
   *and* it means "the group found a chest" doesn't mean "the group found six chests."
2. **Anything craftable-and-repeatable must be evaluated at 6x.** Iron's Spellbooks armor is
   the specific case: its rate is already 5.1x anchor, and it's a *recipe*, so the true
   aggregate rate is catastrophic. This is why it's #1 on the fix list.

### 1.5.3 Effort ≠ hours

Already encoded as T. The practical consequence worth stating plainly: **the Create-tax
complaint from Beyond Depth reviews is not a whine, it is this exact defect.** *"The only
thing you accomplish by implementing the Create mod here is making the game more tedious."*
Grinding andesite to unlock armor is T=1.3 work paying ΔP=0.05. That is R=0.0009 — the worst
number in the entire table, worse than any overpowered sword is over. **Do not repeat it.**

### 1.5.4 Intra-mod RNG variance — the unfairness R cannot see

*(Added by completeness review. `T` covers grind **quality**; nothing in this document covered
grind **luck**, and the two are different defects.)*

R measures a track's **mean**. Your friends compare **outcomes**. Two players can run the same
mod for the same hours with the same skill and walk away with materially different power — and
that is your stated nightmare occurring inside a single mod, immune to every cross-track fix in
Section 2.

| Mod | Variance source | Spread over identical hours | Severity |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Apotheosis** | Affix magnitude is a *range* (`violent` +5.0→+8.0), rarity is RNG, socket count is RNG, gem quality is RNG. Four independent rolls compounding. | **2–3×** | 🔴 **HIGH** |
| **Simply Swords** (unpatched) | Uniques are loot-chest random *and* their procs are chance-based. The Overhaul addon exists **because of this** and does not port to 1.21.1. | ~2× | 🟡 Medium |
| **Iron's Spellbooks** | Whether you ever *see* an Eldritch Blast scroll is luck. Rarity gates which spells exist, not how often they drop. | ~1.8× | 🟡 Medium |
| **Cataclysm** | Kill the boss → get the weapon. **Zero variance.** | 1.0× | 🟢 None |

**Notice the pattern, because it is the whole point:** *the track that overpays is also the
highest-variance one, and the track that underpays is perfectly deterministic.* So Cataclysm's
40 hours reliably buy 0.90–1.35×, while Apotheosis's 20 hours buy anywhere from 2× to 5×.

**And it compounds with §1.5.2 gifting:** the lucky roll gets shared, so **group power is set by
the luckiest roll among six players, not the average one.** Six players farming affixes is six
draws from the tail. With six samples you will hit the 95th percentile roll, not the median —
so the *effective* Apotheosis P is meaningfully higher than any table in this project states.

**Fix:** encoded as **R8**. Narrow ranges rather than lowering ceilings, and convert chance-based
effects to deterministic ones (the §2.2 Overhaul template). **A distribution with a long tail
cannot be balanced by its mean** — already stated in §2.3 about Apotheosis specifically, and it
generalizes to every RNG track in the pack.

### 1.5.5 Unequal hours, latecomers, and the cost of a retroactive nerf

**⚠️ The three things most likely to actually end this campaign are not in this document.**
Every number here assumes six people play the same amount. They won't.

Rate parity *guarantees* that the 20 h/week player ends 5× stronger than the 4 h/week player —
and the pack is working exactly as designed when that happens. Perfect track parity converts a
mod-choice grievance into a **schedule** grievance, which is worse because nobody can fix it by
choosing differently.

**Full treatment — including the latecomer problem and why mid-campaign nerfs cost more goodwill
than the imbalance did — is in `00-MODLIST-AUDIT.md` §0.6.** The three actions that matter:

1. **Capstone bosses are scheduled group events**, not soloable. Free, social, and it removes most
   of the hour-count advantage without any config change.
2. **Diminishing returns per track** — past the capstone, further hours pay in materials and
   cosmetics, not power. Turns "who played most" into "who finished most tracks," which is a
   choice rather than a schedule.
3. **Nerf windows, and close the tap rather than reaching into inventories.** Prefer loot-table
   and drop-rate edits (affect future acquisition) over attribute nerfs (confiscate what someone
   already earned). **This is a second, independent argument for the "fix H, not P" doctrine**
   that §2.0 and §3.5 already recommend on technical grounds — it is also the socially cheaper
   lever, which is a nice coincidence worth exploiting deliberately.

---

# 2. PER-MOD FIXES

## 2.0 The technical constraint nobody tells you

**In 1.21.1 you CANNOT change a modded item's base attack damage with a vanilla datapack.**
Item attribute modifiers live in the `minecraft:attribute_modifiers` **item component**, which
is baked into the item's code-side definition. Datapacks can override recipes, loot tables,
tags, enchantments, and advancements — **not** item components.

So the lever hierarchy, best to worst:

1. **Mod config** — if the mod exposes the number, use it. Zero fragility.
2. **KubeJS `ItemEvents.modification`** — script-side component rewriting. Works, but is a
   scripting dependency and breaks on mod updates.
3. **Loot / recipe removal via datapack** — you can't nerf the item, but you can make it
   rare or unobtainable. Blunt but bulletproof.
4. **AttributeFix global clamp** — hard ceiling on `minecraft:attack_damage` for everything.
   Last resort; it will clip things you didn't intend.
5. **Delete the mod.** Genuinely on the table. A mod whose numbers you cannot reach is a mod
   you cannot balance.

### 1.21 syntax gotchas that will silently break your files

| Thing | Pre-1.21 | **1.21 / 1.21.1** |
|---|---|---|
| Attribute ids | `generic.attack_damage` | **`attack_damage`** (namespaced: `minecraft:attack_damage`) |
| Attribute ids | `generic.max_health` | **`max_health`** |
| Attribute ids | `generic.movement_speed` | **`movement_speed`** |
| Modifier operations | `addition` / `multiply_base` / `multiply_total` | **`add_value` / `add_multiplied_base` / `add_multiplied_total`** |
| Data folder | `data/<ns>/recipes/` | **`data/<ns>/recipe/`** (singular) |
| Data folder | `data/<ns>/loot_tables/` | **`data/<ns>/loot_table/`** |
| Data folder | `data/<ns>/advancements/` | **`data/<ns>/advancement/`** |
| Data folder | `data/<ns>/tags/items/` | **`data/<ns>/tags/item/`** |
| Data folder | `data/<ns>/predicates/` | **`data/<ns>/predicate/`** |
| Data folder | `data/<ns>/functions/` | **`data/<ns>/function/`** |
| Recipe result | `"result": {"item": "x"}` | **`"result": {"id": "x", "count": 1}`** |
| Enchantments | hardcoded | **fully data-driven** at `data/<ns>/enchantment/*.json` |

⚠ **VERIFY BEFORE APPLYING — two format questions I am not certain about on 1.21.1
specifically:**
- **Ingredient flattening.** In some 1.21.x version, `{"item": "minecraft:stick"}` became just
  `"minecraft:stick"` in recipe ingredients. I believe the flattened form is **1.21.2+** and
  that **1.21.1 still uses the object form**, but confirm by opening a vanilla recipe out of
  the 1.21.1 server jar (`data/minecraft/recipe/stick.json`) before writing twenty files.
- **Attribute modifier identity.** In 1.21/1.21.1 the `minecraft:attribute_modifiers` component
  uses `uuid` (int array) + `name` (string). In 1.21.2+ those were collapsed into a single
  namespaced `id` string. If you write KubeJS attribute modifiers, get this right or they
  silently no-op. Confirm the same way: dump a vanilla diamond sword's components with
  `/data get entity @s SelectedItem`.

**Do not skip that check. It costs two minutes and saves a debugging evening.**

---

## 2.1 🔴 Iron's Spellbooks (+ Ars Nouveau + Ars 'n Spells) — HIGHEST PRIORITY

**Defect:** `R ≈ 0.056`, **5.1x anchor**, and it's on the *same reward axis* as Ars Nouveau,
which sits at 0.0117. Two players both "do magic," one ends up twice as strong. This is the
owner's stated nightmare in its purest form.

**Root cause — verified from source** (`AbstractSpell.java`, branch `1.21`):

```java
return (float) ((baseSpellPower + spellPowerPerLevel * (spellLevel - 1))
    * entitySpellPowerModifier * entitySchoolPowerModifier * configPowerModifier);
```

The general modifier and the school modifier **multiply**. `AttributeRegistry.java` confirms
both are `MagicPercentAttribute(..., 1.0D, -100, 100)` — base 1.0, so +5% gear = 1.05.

Committed single-school gear stack (armor % from iron.wiki, `MAX_UPGRADES` default **3**
confirmed in `ServerConfigs.java`):

```
4× school armor      : +40% school, +20% general
School spellbook     : +10% school
Upgrade orbs @ +5% × 3 per item
                       ────────────────────────
General  ≈ 1.20+
School   ≈ 1.50+
Product  ≈ 1.20 × 1.50 = 1.80x   ← and that's before orbs
With orbs, comfortably >2.4x
```

**Multiplicative stacking is the whole story.** Additive would cap this at ~1.7x; multiplicative
has no ceiling.

### Fix A — install Ars 'n Spells and tighten its cap (RECOMMENDED)

Ars 'n Spells is **itself a balance mod** and it already made this exact correction. From its
CHANGELOG v1.7.0:

> *"Spell scaling changed from multiplicative to additive — Iron's base spell power and
> elemental spell power bonuses are now added instead of multiplied, preventing exponential
> stacking"*

plus a hard `spell_power_cap`, default **3.0**.

**Verified availability:** `3.2.2`, CurseForge, 2026-08-28. Requires MC 1.21.1 / NeoForge
21.1.84+ / Java 21 / Ars Nouveau 5.11.1+ / Iron's Spellbooks 1.21.1-3.16.0+.

**Config file:** `config/ars_n_spells-common.toml`
⚠ **VERIFY** — filename inferred from NeoForge convention. Boot the server once and look at
what appears in `config/`.

```toml
# Default 3.0 is far too permissive. 3.0x spell power against an 18.0 DPS
# netherite baseline is P=3.0 → R=0.08 → 7x anchor.
# 1.40 puts a 25-hour magic track at R = 0.40/25 = 0.016, top of the green band.
spell_power_cap = 1.40

# Keep additive stacking. This is the single most important line in the file.
# (Key name unverified — the CHANGELOG describes the behavior, not the key.
#  Look for anything named *_additive / *_stacking / *_scaling_mode.)

# Unify mana so the two magic tracks are one economy, not two.
enable_mana_unification = true
mana_unification_mode = "iss_primary"

# Cross-casting should cost something, or hybrid becomes strictly optimal.
# Default 1.25 → 1.40.
cross_cast_cost_multiplier = 1.40

# Default 5.0 is a large passive regen multiplier and it stacks with everything.
source_jar_synergy_multiplier = 2.50

# Resonance: flat damage bonus while mana >95%. On a 6-player server with
# downtime between fights, players will *always* be above 95% at engagement.
# Either disable, or lower the threshold gate so it's actually a resource decision.
# ⚠ VERIFY key names — README lists the mechanic, not the exact keys.
```

⚠ **Repo/binary drift:** the public `port/neoforge-1.21.1` branch README and CHANGELOG top out
at **3.0.2** while CurseForge ships **3.2.2**. You cannot currently audit what's in the jar you'd
deploy. GPLv3 means source is owed, so it may catch up. Minor risk, not a blocker — but it does
mean **you must dump the generated config and read it rather than trusting the README key list.**

### Fix B — if Ars 'n Spells is rejected, nerf Iron's directly

**Config file:** `config/irons_spellbooks-server.toml`
⚠ **VERIFY** — path and key names both unconfirmed.

```toml
# ServerConfigs.MAX_UPGRADES, default 3. Each orb is +5% and they multiply
# into the school modifier. Cutting to 1 removes ~2/3 of the compounding.
maxUpgrades = 1

# Global config power modifier — the `configPowerModifier` term in the source
# formula above. This is the one clean global scalar.
# 0.65 brings a 2.40x stack down to ~1.56x → ΔP 0.56 / 25h = R 0.022.
# 0.55 → ~1.32x → R 0.013, dead centre of band. Start at 0.55.
spellPowerMultiplier = 0.55
```

**Verification:** in creative, equip the full school set, then
`/attribute @s irons_spellbooks:fire_magic_resist get` (and the spell power attributes — check
`/attribute @s ` tab-completion for the real ids). Cast a known spell at a test dummy and read
the damage. **Compare against a Sharpness V netherite sword's 18.0 DPS, not against your
memory of what felt fine.**

---

## 2.2 🔴 Simply Swords — the Overhaul does not port

**Verified problem:** Simply Swords base mod is alive on 1.21.1 NeoForge
(`simplyswords-neoforge-1.70.2-1.21.1.jar`, 2026-08-27). **Simply Swords: Overhaul — the
dedicated rebalance addon — is 1.20.1 Forge only and will never port.**

That is significant, because the Overhaul exists *because the base mod needed it*. Beyond Depth
shipped it. You cannot.

**What the Overhaul actually did** (this is the *template* for every fix in this pack):

| Weapon | Original | Overhaul |
|---|---|---|
| The Watcher / Watching Warglaive | chance-based lifesteal | **deterministic**: 10% victim max HP on kill, 0.5 HP/hit |
| Storm's Edge | RNG cooldown reduction | **guaranteed** 1s/hit; dash simplified to 12 blocks |
| Soulrender | inconsistent debuffs | consistent; +2→+4 dmg per stack; **healing capped at 50% max HP** |
| Soul Pyre | complex teleport/freeze | Wither II on hit + 32-block pull |
| Molten Edge | — | scales per 20% missing HP; active costs 4 HP for 10s Regen |
| Whisperwind | passive reset on any hit | **resets only on kills**; dash crit to 22 blocks |
| Star's Edge | scaling passive | **fixed at 3 magic damage** |

**Read the pattern: RNG → deterministic, and hard caps on anything that scales.** That is
exactly the correction this pack needs, generalized.

### Options, in order of preference

**Option 1 — Simply Swords: Cataclysm (INVESTIGATE FIRST).**
`Simply Cataclysm 1.0.1` exists for NeoForge 1.21.1. It's a *different* bridge than the
Overhaul (Cataclysm integration rather than rebalance), but it's the only live companion mod.
⚠ **VERIFY** whether it touches unique-weapon balance at all. It may be pure content. Twenty
minutes of reading its page decides this.

**Option 2 — config the uniques down.**
**Config file:** `config/simplyswords-common.toml` or `config/simplyswords.json`
⚠ **VERIFY** — I do not know which, and I do not know whether unique-effect magnitudes are
exposed at all. Boot once, read `config/`. If the magnitudes aren't exposed, skip to 3 or 4.

**Option 3 — cut the uniques out of loot (RECOMMENDED FALLBACK, bulletproof).**
The problem with Simply Swords isn't the ceiling, it's `H = 8`. R = 0.0625 because these are
**loot-chest random**, not earned. Fix the H, not the P: move uniques from generic structure
loot onto **boss drops only**. Datapack, no scripting, no mod-update fragility. See §3.4 for
the loot-table pattern.

**Option 4 — remove the mod.** The base mod's value is weapon *variety*, which is largely
cosmetic-adjacent. If it costs you a week of tuning, it isn't worth it. Say so out loud.

---

## 2.3 🔴 Apotheosis / Apothic Attributes — the unbounded affix ceiling

**Defect:** `R ≈ 0.042`, 3.8x anchor. Worse, affix gear has **no ceiling** — it's an RNG
distribution, so with six players farming, someone eventually rolls the tail.

**A distribution with a long tail cannot be balanced by its mean.** You must clamp the tail.

**Verified availability:** Apothic Attributes `2.10.1`, 1.21.1 NeoForge, 2026-07-24.

**⚠ KNOWN HARD CONFLICT — test this before you build anything on it:**
`Conflicting default methods: dev/architectury/registry/registries/DeferredSupplier.getKey`
vs `net/neoforged/neoforge/common/extensions/IHolderExtension.getKey`. Reported against
Apotheosis 1.21.1-8.1.2 / Architectury 13.0.8 / NeoForge 21.1.94,
[architectury issue #592](https://github.com/architectury/architectury-api/issues/592),
**still open**. Architectury 13.0.11 (2026-07) is newer than the reported version so it may
already be fixed. **Verify, don't assume.** If it crashes and Apotheosis is load-bearing for
your progression, you have a mod-selection decision, not a balance decision.

### The fix: cut affix tiers, not affix existence

**Config directory:** `config/apotheosis/` — Apotheosis uses a directory of JSON/TOML files
(affixes, gems, spawner, adventure). ⚠ **VERIFY** exact filenames on 1.21.1.

Targets, in priority order:
1. **Remove or heavily downweight the top rarity tier** (Ancient/Mythic — whatever the top
   band is called on this version). The tail is the problem; deleting the tail is the fix.
2. **Halve `attack_damage` affix magnitudes.** Anything expressed as
   `add_multiplied_total` on `minecraft:attack_damage` is the compounding term — that's where
   the 2.0x comes from.
3. **Reduce affix-item drop rate.** This raises H, which is the cheaper half of the R equation
   and is much less likely to break the mod's feel than magnitude nerfs.
4. **Keep the utility affixes at full strength.** They pay on a different axis (R4) and they're
   what makes Apotheosis fun. Nerf damage, keep flavor.

### Global backstop: AttributeFix

**Config file:** `config/attributefix.json`
⚠ **VERIFY** — key format on 1.21.1. AttributeFix's job in Beyond Depth was *raising* caps so
stacking mods didn't overflow. You're using it backwards: **as a hard ceiling.**

```jsonc
// Conceptual shape — real file is per-attribute objects with min/max/enabled.
// Remember: 1.21 dropped the `generic.` prefix.
{
  "minecraft:attack_damage": { "min": 0.0, "max": 20.0, "enabled": true },
  "minecraft:max_health":    { "min": 1.0, "max": 40.0, "enabled": true },
  "minecraft:armor":         { "min": 0.0, "max": 30.0, "enabled": true }
}
```

`max: 20.0` on attack damage is the R2 ceiling (13) plus enchantment headroom (Sharp V's
+3.25) plus a small margin. **This is a blunt instrument** — it will clamp things you didn't
intend, including possibly boss mobs. Use it as a safety net *after* the targeted fixes, and
test it hard.

---

## 2.4 🔵 Create — the under-rewarding defect (BUFF / DE-GATE, do not nerf)

`R = 0.0009`, **12x under floor**. This is the worst number in the table and it is a
*generosity* failure, not a power failure.

**This is not a config fix. It is a design fix, and it is one line:**

> **Create must never be a prerequisite for combat gear.**

Beyond Depth's single clearest design error, straight from its reviews: *"The only thing you
accomplish by implementing the Create mod here is making the game more tedious."* Grinding
andesite to unlock armor converts a beloved sandbox into a chore. **Rule R6 exists specifically
to prevent this.**

### Actions

1. **Audit every armor and weapon recipe for Create-only ingredients.** In JEI, search each
   tier and look for `create:` namespace items in the tree. If you find any on a combat-critical
   item, add an alternative non-Create recipe via datapack (§3.3).
2. **Reframe Create in the track menu as an economy track, not a power track** (§4). Its ΔP is
   ~0.05 and always will be — that's fine, because it's paying in a currency the R metric
   doesn't measure. **Exempt Create from R1/R5 explicitly** and say so in the menu so the
   Create player knows they signed up for infrastructure, not damage.
3. **If Create is going to be a "track," it needs a combat-axis capstone** so its player isn't
   permanently 12x behind everyone. Options: a Create-craftable weapon at ~11–13 damage, or
   Create-gated *access* to another track's rewards (e.g. a machine that duplicates a
   boss-drop consumable). ⚠ This is a design decision, not a config — flag for Wesley.

---

## 2.5 ✅ L_Ender's Cataclysm — the anchor. Change almost nothing.

**Verified availability:** `1.21.1-3.33.jar`, NeoForge, 2026-08-22. 111M downloads. Actively
maintained. This is the healthiest mod in the pack.

**Verified stats — this table is your yardstick, keep it visible:**

| Item | Damage | Speed | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Infernal Forge | **13** | **1.0** | Unbreakable, enchantability 15, netherite-tier pickaxe. R-click: 13-dmg AoE slam, knockback, breaks shields in 4-block radius, 4s CD (configurable). Drops from Netherite Monstrosity. |
| Void Forge | **13** | **1.0** | Infernal Forge + Void Core at Mechanical Fusion Anvil. R-click: 35 Void Runes in 8-block fan, 1.7s, 7 magic dmg, hits twice. |

| Boss | HP | Armor | Toughness | Damage |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Netherite Monstrosity | **600** | 12 | 5 | **25** (100% knockback resist) |
| Ancient Remnant | **450** | 12 | 4 | ⚠ wiki says `???` |
| Ender Guardian | **333** | 20 → 15 (phase 2) | — | **16** |

For scale: vanilla Wither 300 HP, Ender Dragon 200 HP. **Cataclysm's difficulty lives in boss
HP and mechanics, not in player gear.** That is the correct posture and you should copy it.

### The one change to consider: 6-player boss scaling

600 HP and 25 damage was tuned for a smaller group. Six players with Sharp V netherite will
delete the Netherite Monstrosity. If the anchor track becomes trivial, **every R in the table
recalculates downward** — you'd be balancing against a broken yardstick.

**Config file:** `config/cataclysm-common.toml` ⚠ **VERIFY** path and whether boss HP is
exposed at all (cooldowns are, per the wiki's "configurable" note on the Forge slam).

If boss HP is not exposed, the alternatives are:
- A difficulty-scaling mod (Beyond Depth used Majrusz's Progressive Difficulty for this —
  **it is dead on 1.21.1**, last update 2026-04-26, max 1.20.1, and there is **no identified
  1.21.1 equivalent**. This is the biggest structural loss in the reconstruction and you should
  tell the group about it explicitly.)
- Accept it, and instead **lower every other track's P** so the anchor stays the anchor.
- Re-derive the anchor at the observed 6-player H (if the gauntlet takes 25h with six people
  instead of 40, then `R* = 0.35/(25×0.8) = 0.0175` and the whole band shifts up).

**That last option is the honest one and it is a Section 6.1 measurement task.** Do not skip it —
the entire band is derived from `H = 40`, and if the real number is 25, every "🔴 5x anchor"
verdict in this doc becomes "3x anchor."

---

## 2.6 🟡 Artifacts / Relics — axis mismatch, not a magnitude problem

`R = 0.0061` — inside the band, low half. **Do not nerf.** The problem is R4: it pays in
mobility/utility while its advertised siblings pay in damage.

**Verified:** Artifacts `artifacts-neoforge-13.2.3.jar` for 1.21.1 ✅.
Relics 1.21.1 status **unverified** — the CurseForge slug 404'd during recon. Check before
including it.

**Fixes:**
1. **Menu framing (§4).** Advertise the trinket track as a swap for *exploration/utility*
   tracks, never for boss tracks. Half of a perceived-unfairness problem is solved by
   accurate advertising.
2. **Curios, not Accessories.** Curios is the correct spine on 1.21.1 — it's frozen at
   `9.5.1+1.21.1` (2025-05-14) because the API is *done*, not abandoned (the project itself is
   active through 26.2). Iron's Spellbooks requires a Curios spellbook slot, which settles it.
   Only add Accessories if a mod you actually want is Accessories-native, and if you do, bridge
   with **Accessories Compatibility Layer** (`0.1.11`, 1.21.1, NeoForge) — never run both APIs
   bare, that gives players two separate accessory screens.
   **Do NOT install Curios API Continuation** — unofficial fork, last updated 2024-11-09,
   strictly worse than upstream.
3. **Gifting (R5):** slot-limited, so effective divisor is ~2–3, not 6. Acceptable. No action.

---

## 2.7 ✅ Dimension tracks — Undergarden / Deeper and Darker / Voidscape

All three land in the green band. All three verified on 1.21.1 NeoForge:

| Mod | Version evidence | R |
|---|---|---|
| The Undergarden | Modrinth 1.21–1.21.1, Forge+NeoForge, `26.1.2`, updated days ago | 0.0083 |
| Deeper and Darker | Modrinth 1.21–1.21.1, multi-loader, ~2 months old | 0.0126 |
| Voidscape | `Voidscape-1.21.1-1.9.588.jar`, NeoForge, 2026-05-31 | 0.0091 |

**No fixes required.** These are the shape the pack wants: distinct place, distinct loot,
proportionate payoff.

**Watch item:** Undergarden at 0.0083 vs Deeper and Darker at 0.0126 is a 1.5x gap on the *same
axis*. That is inside the band but at the edge of noticeable. If players complain, the fix is to
**shorten Undergarden**, not to buff it — raising P raises the ceiling for everyone downstream.

**Do NOT use Restricted Portals to hard-sequence these.** Beyond Depth used it, and it's the
right tool for a linear pack — but the owner explicitly rejected forced linear progression, and
hard-gating three parallel dimension tracks against each other destroys the hub premise.
Recommend it **only** if playtesting shows a track is genuinely broken without it.

---

## 2.8 ⚠ "Antarchy" — the named offender I have no data on

The owner named this as the mod whose numbers broke the curve (the 30-damage sword). **The
research payload for this doc was empty and I have no primary source on Antarchy at all** — not
a version, not a config path, not a confirmation it exists on 1.21.1.

**Marked entirely `⚠ VERIFY BEFORE APPLYING`. Do not act on the row 12 numbers.**

**The measurement procedure (do this before any fix):**

```
1. Creative world, single mod + deps.
2. /give @s <antarchy:item>
3. Hold it. Run:  /attribute @s minecraft:attack_damage get
   → this reads YOUR total damage including the held item's modifier.
4. Dump the item's real components:  /data get entity @s SelectedItem
   → read minecraft:attribute_modifiers directly. This is ground truth.
5. Attack speed: check the same component for minecraft:attack_speed,
   or count swings-to-full-charge on a test dummy.
6. Compute: DPS = damage × speed.  P = DPS / 18.0.
7. In JEI, look up the item → note every recipe/loot source.
   Estimate H honestly. Do not estimate it optimistically.
8. R = (P − 1.0) / (H × T). Compare against 0.005–0.018.
```

**Then, and only then, pick a lever from §2.0.** If the mod has no config for the number and
KubeJS feels like too much fragility, the answer is **loot removal or mod removal**, and that
is a completely legitimate answer.

---

## 2.9 Infrastructure to install on day one, not after the imbalance shows up

Beyond Depth's whole balance toolchain, filtered to what's confirmed on 1.21.1:

| Mod | Job | Status |
|---|---|---|
| **KubeJS** (+ Rhino) | Item component / recipe scripting — the only way to touch modded item stats | Confirmed in the 1.21.1 ecosystem |
| **LootJS** | Programmatic loot rewriting — the H-lever | Confirmed |
| **Paxi** | Ship your balance datapack **globally**, auto-loads into any world from `config/paxi/datapacks/`, with `datapack_load_order.json` | 1.20–1.21.1+, 47.4M downloads ✅ |
| **Lootr** | Per-player structure chests — **kills the R5 gifting divisor for loot** | Confirmed ✅ |
| **In Control!** | JSON rules engine for spawns — shape *which* mobs *where*, never a global multiplier | Confirmed |
| **AttributeFix** | Global attribute clamp (§2.3) | Confirmed |
| **Enchantment Blacklister** | Surgical enchantment removal | Confirmed |
| **Structurify / Sparse Structures Reforged** | Structure rarity = the H-lever for exploration tracks | Confirmed |
| **spark** | Profiler. **Non-negotiable** on a 32GB box that's also a gaming rig | `1.10.124-neoforge-1.21.1` ✅ |
| **Chunky** | Pre-generate spawn region before launch night | `1.4.23` ✅ |

**Install these before the modlist is finalized.** Retrofitting KubeJS into a running campaign
is how you end up not fixing things.

**Not needed on NeoForge:** Forge Config API Port (built in). Harmless if a jar drags it in.

---

## 2.10 Raise the vanilla baseline (cheap, high-value, often skipped)

Beyond Depth buffs Wither / Ender Dragon / Warden so modded content lands in the right band.
This fixes the relative-power problem **from the other end** and it's much less invasive than
nerfing ten mods.

Six players will delete a 300 HP Wither. If vanilla bosses are trivial, every modded boss feels
mandatory and every modded reward feels required.

⚠ **Constraint:** vanilla entity base attributes are **code-side in 1.21.1** — a datapack cannot
change Wither HP. Your options are content mods (Beyond Depth used Savage Ender Dragon, True
Ending, Wither: Reincarnated, Boss Refreshed — ⚠ 1.21.1 status **unverified for all four**,
check before planning around them) or KubeJS entity attribute events.

**What a datapack CAN do here, and should:**
- **Rewrite vanilla boss loot tables** (`data/minecraft/loot_table/entities/wither.json`) so the
  vanilla bosses pay competitively with modded ones. This is a real, free lever most people miss.
- **Nerf Sharpness** via data-driven enchantments (§3.5). If Sharp V is +3.25, the netherite
  baseline is 18.0 DPS and every modded weapon is measured against that. Lowering Sharpness
  lowers the whole curve without touching a single mod.

---

# 3. THE DATAPACK SKELETON

## 3.1 Where it lives

Two options. **Use both.**

```
# Per-world (authoritative, version-controlled with the world):
C:\Game Servers\Minecraft\server\world\datapacks\bd-balance\

# Global via Paxi (survives world regen, applies to test worlds too):
C:\Game Servers\Minecraft\server\config\paxi\datapacks\bd-balance\
```

Paxi is the reason you can iterate: drop the same folder in `config/paxi/datapacks/` and it
auto-loads into **every** world including your creative test world, so what you test is what
ships. Control ordering with `config/paxi/datapack_load_order.json`.

## 3.2 Folder structure

```
bd-balance\
├── pack.mcmeta
└── data\
    ├── minecraft\
    │   ├── enchantment\
    │   │   ├── sharpness.json              # nerf the baseline (§3.5)
    │   │   └── protection.json
    │   ├── loot_table\
    │   │   └── entities\
    │   │       ├── wither.json             # buff vanilla boss payout (§2.10)
    │   │       └── ender_dragon.json
    │   └── tags\
    │       └── item\
    │           └── enchantable\
    │               └── sharp_weapon.json   # control what CAN take Sharpness
    │
    ├── bdbalance\                          # your own namespace
    │   ├── recipe\                         # replacement / alternative recipes
    │   ├── loot_table\
    │   │   └── injection\                  # tables you reference from mod tables
    │   ├── predicate\
    │   ├── item_modifier\
    │   ├── function\
    │   │   └── debug\
    │   │       └── parity_kit.mcfunction   # §6 testing kit
    │   └── advancement\
    │
    ├── simplyswords\
    │   └── loot_table\chests\...           # relocate uniques to boss drops (§2.2)
    │
    ├── apotheosis\
    │   └── recipe\...                      # kill specific recipes (§3.3)
    │
    └── <every other mod you override>\
        ├── recipe\
        └── loot_table\
```

**The rule:** to override a mod's file, mirror its **exact namespace and path**. To override
`simplyswords:chests/dungeon_uniques`, your file goes at
`data/simplyswords/loot_table/chests/dungeon_uniques.json`. World datapacks load after
mod-provided data, so yours wins.

## 3.3 `pack.mcmeta`

```json
{
  "pack": {
    "pack_format": 48,
    "supported_formats": { "min_inclusive": 48, "max_inclusive": 48 },
    "description": "Beyond Depth Reconstruction — pack-wide balance overrides"
  }
}
```

**`pack_format 48` is correct for 1.21 and 1.21.1.** (1.21.2–1.21.3 is 57. If you ever bump the
game version, this number changes and a stale value produces a scary-but-harmless "made for a
different version" warning — or, worse, silent format mismatches.)

## 3.4 Killing a recipe on NeoForge

Vanilla datapacks can't *delete* a recipe, only overwrite it. NeoForge gives you a clean
condition instead. Put this at the **exact path of the recipe you're killing**:

```json
{
  "neoforge:conditions": [
    { "type": "neoforge:false" }
  ],
  "type": "minecraft:crafting_shaped",
  "pattern": ["X"],
  "key": {
    "X": { "item": "minecraft:stone" }
  },
  "result": { "id": "minecraft:stone", "count": 1 }
}
```

The condition evaluates false, so the recipe never loads. The body is inert filler — it just has
to parse. ⚠ **VERIFY the ingredient form** (`{"item": "..."}` vs bare `"..."`) against a vanilla
1.21.1 recipe before mass-producing these; see §2.0.

## 3.5 Relocating loot (the H-lever, and your most reliable tool)

You cannot nerf a modded sword's damage from a datapack. You **can** make it cost 30 hours
instead of 8. For Simply Swords uniques (§2.2), overwrite the generic structure table so the
unique isn't in it:

```json
{
  "type": "minecraft:chest",
  "pools": [
    {
      "rolls": 3,
      "entries": [
        { "type": "minecraft:item", "name": "minecraft:iron_ingot", "weight": 20,
          "functions": [
            { "function": "minecraft:set_count",
              "count": { "min": 2.0, "max": 6.0 } }
          ]
        },
        { "type": "minecraft:item", "name": "minecraft:diamond", "weight": 4 },
        { "type": "minecraft:empty", "weight": 10 }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

…and add it to a boss entity table instead. Same JSON shape, `"type": "minecraft:entity"`,
usually with a `minecraft:killed_by_player` condition so it can't be farmed by fall damage.

⚠ **VERIFY** the exact table id in-game. Guessing loot-table paths from mod names is how you
spend an hour wondering why nothing changed. Use `/loot` to test:
`/loot give @s loot simplyswords:chests/whatever` — if it errors, the path is wrong.

## 3.6 Data-driven enchantments (new in 1.21 — use this)

Enchantments are fully datapack-controlled in 1.21. Nerfing Sharpness lowers the entire power
curve without touching one mod file. Template at
`data/minecraft/enchantment/sharpness.json`:

```json
{
  "description": { "translate": "enchantment.minecraft.sharpness" },
  "supported_items": "#minecraft:enchantable/sword",
  "primary_items": "#minecraft:enchantable/sharp_weapon",
  "weight": 10,
  "max_level": 4,
  "min_cost": { "base": 1, "per_level_above_first": 11 },
  "max_cost": { "base": 21, "per_level_above_first": 11 },
  "anvil_cost": 1,
  "slots": ["mainhand"],
  "effects": {
    "minecraft:damage": [
      {
        "effect": {
          "type": "minecraft:add",
          "value": {
            "type": "minecraft:linear",
            "base": 1.0,
            "per_level_above_first": 0.5
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

Vanilla Sharpness is `max_level: 5` with `base 1.0 / per_level 0.5` → +3.0 at V (the +3.25
figure used elsewhere in this doc is the commonly-cited value; **verify which is right for
1.21.1 by reading the vanilla file out of the server jar**). Dropping to `max_level: 4` cuts the
top enchant and pulls the netherite baseline from 18.0 to ~17.2 DPS.

⚠ **VERIFY** the exact effect component names against the vanilla 1.21.1 file. Extract the
server jar, look in `data/minecraft/enchantment/`, and copy its structure rather than mine —
this is a new format and I am working from memory of it.

**Extraction command** (this is the highest-leverage 30 seconds in the whole doc):

```powershell
# Adjust the jar name/path to your actual server jar
$jar = "C:\Game Servers\Minecraft\server\minecraft_server.1.21.1.jar"
$out = "$env:TEMP\mc-vanilla-data"
Expand-Archive -Path $jar -DestinationPath $out -Force
# Now read the real files:
#   $out\data\minecraft\enchantment\sharpness.json
#   $out\data\minecraft\recipe\stick.json          <- settles the ingredient-format question
#   $out\data\minecraft\loot_table\entities\wither.json
```

**Every "⚠ VERIFY" on 1.21.1 JSON syntax in this document is answered by that one command.**

---

# 4. THE TRACK MENU
### *(paste this straight into Discord — this is the whole point)*

---

**📋 SERVER TRACK MENU — pick with open eyes**

We're running a **hub**, not a ladder. There's no "right order." Everybody picks what looks fun
and we all end up roughly equal in power. Tracks are grouped by **how much time they want**, not
by when you're supposed to do them.

Two things to know up front:

**1. "Roughly equal" means equal *per hour*, not equal *in total*.** A 40-hour track pays more
than a 15-hour track. That's not unfair, that's just more hours. What we've tuned for is that
hour 20 of your track is worth about as much as hour 20 of mine.

**2. Tracks pay in different currencies.** Some give damage, some give mobility, some give a
factory. Check the "fair swap for" line before you start, so you're not 30 hours in wondering
why your friend hits harder. If you want to hit hard, pick a hitting-hard track.

**3. Some tracks don't pay in power at all, and that's on purpose.** They're in the **SPECIAL**
section at the bottom. If you pick one, you are choosing a different game, not a slower version
of this one. **Read that section before you commit 60 hours to it.** Nobody should find this out
in month three.

**4. The big bosses are group events, not races.** We're scheduling them. Not because you can't
solo them, but because some of us get 4 hours a week and some get 20, and there's no config file
that fixes that — only a calendar. **If the capstone fights happen when we're all on, nobody
falls behind for having a job.**

**5. How balance changes work, so nobody gets robbed.** If something needs tuning, it happens in
**the first two weeks, or at an announced monthly patch** — never as a surprise mid-week. And
where possible we change **how often a thing drops**, not how strong the one in your chest
already is. **If we ever have to nerf something you already earned, you get something equivalent
back.** You will not lose hours you already spent.

If anything below feels wrong once you're in it, **say so in #server-chat immediately.** Tuning
mid-campaign is fine. Being quietly annoyed for a month is not.

---

### ☕ LIGHT — 10–20 hours

**🗺️ Structure Explorer** *(YUNG's suite + Towns and Towers)*
Every vanilla dungeon, mineshaft, stronghold and temple rebuilt into an actual dungeon.
Wander, loot, come home rich.
→ **~20h.** Walk away with: a full kit of good gear, a mountain of materials, a map full of pins.
→ *Fair swap for:* Trinket Hunter, End Run.

**💍 Trinket Hunter** *(Artifacts + Relics)*
Collect passive-effect trinkets. Wings, boots, rings, weird little items that change how you move.
→ **~15h.** Walk away with: **mobility and utility, not raw damage.** You will be the person who
gets there first and never takes fall damage. You will *not* be the person who one-shots things.
→ *Fair swap for:* Structure Explorer. ⚠ **Not** a swap for a boss track — different currency.

**🐉 End Run** *(End Remastered + the Dragon)*
Hunt down 16 custom Eyes across the world, place 12, open the portal, kill the Dragon.
A scavenger hunt with a boss at the end.
→ **~15h.** Walk away with: elytra, dragon loot, the End opened for everyone.
→ *Fair swap for:* Structure Explorer, Undergarden.

---

### 🍺 MEDIUM — 20–30 hours

**🍄 The Undergarden** *(Undergarden)*
A full second underworld. New biomes, new mobs, its own materials and gear tier.
→ **~18h.** Walk away with: a distinct armor/weapon set, unique building blocks, a place that's yours.
→ *Fair swap for:* Deeper and Darker, End Run.

**🕳️ Deeper and Darker** *(Deeper and Darker)*
Ancient Cities stop being a curiosity and become a destination. Sneak, survive, cross into the
Otherside.
→ **~22h.** Walk away with: strong gear, Warden-tier bragging rights, genuinely tense memories.
→ *Fair swap for:* Undergarden, Ars Nouveau.

**✨ Ars Nouveau** *(Ars Nouveau)*
Build your own spells from glyphs. Deeply customizable, huge utility ceiling, real learning curve.
→ **~30h.** Walk away with: custom spells nobody else has, strong utility, solid combat.
→ **Note:** spell knowledge is **per-player** and can't be gifted. This is the most
"actually yours" track on the menu.
→ *Fair swap for:* Deeper and Darker, Voidscape.

**🌌 Voidscape** *(Voidscape)*
Corrupted floating islands. Hostile, weird, high-payout.
→ **~30h.** Walk away with: endgame-tier gear and the strangest screenshots on the server.
→ *Fair swap for:* Ars Nouveau, Cataclysm (if you're only doing part of Cataclysm).

---

### 🔥 HEAVY — 35+ hours

**⚔️ Cataclysm Boss Gauntlet** *(L_Ender's Cataclysm)*
Eight bosses in eight dungeons. The Netherite Monstrosity has **600 HP, hits for 25, and cannot
be knocked back.** This is the hardest content on the server.
→ **~40h.** Walk away with: the **Void Forge** — 13 damage, unbreakable, doubles as a netherite
pickaxe, and a right-click that fires 35 void runes.
→ **Reality check:** the Void Forge is a **sidegrade** on paper — a Sharpness V netherite sword
does slightly more raw DPS. What you're buying is the AoE, the shield-break, the unbreakability,
and the fact that you killed the thing. **If you want a big number on a tooltip, this is not
that track.** If you want the hardest fights in the pack, it's the only one.
→ **Bosses are re-fightable**, so a second person can get their own Forge without doing less work.
→ *Fair swap for:* Voidscape + one Light track, or Ars Nouveau + one Light track.

**🔮 Iron's Spellbooks** *(Iron's Spellbooks + Ars 'n Spells)*
Structured magic. Pick a school, commit to it, gear up around it.
→ **~25h.** Walk away with: heavy ranged damage and a spell wheel.
→ ⚠ **Currently being tuned before launch.** Raw, this track pays roughly **twice** what
everything else does per hour, which is exactly the thing we're trying not to have. It ships with
a power cap. If it still feels absurd after 10 hours, tell me and I'll pull the cap down again.
→ *Fair swap for:* Ars Nouveau (same currency — these two should feel equal, flag it if they don't).

---

### 🏭 SPECIAL — measured differently

**⚙️ Create Engineering** *(Create + addons)*
Contraptions, automation, trains, absurd machines.
→ **Open-ended.** Walk away with: **infrastructure, not power.** You will not out-damage anyone.
You will make everyone else's life dramatically easier and build things that make people say
"how."
→ **This track is explicitly NOT balanced against the others and that's deliberate.** It pays in
economy and creativity. **Nothing anyone else needs is locked behind it** — if you find a weapon
or armor recipe that requires Create parts, that's a bug, tell me.
→ *Fair swap for:* nothing. It's its own thing. Pick it because contraptions are the fun, not
because you want gear.

**🔴 Cobblemon** *(if it ships — status TBD)*
Catch, train and battle creatures. It is a **complete separate game** running inside our server,
with its own 100-hour progression and its own combat system.
→ **60–100h+, open-ended.** Walk away with: a team, a collection, and **essentially no survival
power.** Your Pokémon do not help you fight the Netherite Monstrosity.
→ **This track is NOT balanced against the others and cannot be.** It pays in a currency nothing
else in the pack uses. **If you pick it, pick it because catching things is the fun.** If you
also want to be useful in a boss fight, run a short combat track alongside it — that's expected
and encouraged, not cheating.
→ *Fair swap for:* nothing. Same deal as Create.

**🔴 Jurassic Reborn** *(if it ships — status TBD)*
Incubate, breed and house dinosaurs. Base-building and spectacle.
→ **40–60h.** Walk away with: **a park, not a weapon.** Same warning as Cobblemon.
→ ⚠️ Also the heaviest thing in the pack for server performance — if it ships there will be a cap
on how many dinos can exist, and that cap is not negotiable. Ask before you plan a mega-paddock.
→ *Fair swap for:* nothing.

> **Why these two get a scary red circle:** not because they're bad — they're great. Because they
> are the two tracks where someone could sink 60+ hours and *then* discover the reward doesn't
> convert. Saying it here, loudly, in advance, is the entire fix.

---

**⚠️ One honest heads-up about the pack itself:** this is a *reconstruction* of Beyond Depth on
a newer Minecraft version, not a copy. Some mods that defined the original — Alex's Caves,
Alex's Mobs, the progressive-difficulty system — **do not exist on 1.21.1** and there's no
replacement. It'll feel different. Mostly it means fewer weird cave biomes and less automatic
difficulty ramp. Setting expectations now so nobody's surprised in week two.

---

# 5. WHAT TO FIX FIRST

Ordered by **impact ÷ effort**. Direction is labeled on every line. **This is not a nerf list —
three of the top ten are buffs or de-gates, and they matter just as much.**

| # | Direction | Fix | Effort | Impact | Why here |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **1** | 🔬 **MEASURE** | **Extract the vanilla 1.21.1 server jar data folder** (§3.6 command) | 5 min | ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ | Answers every `⚠ VERIFY` JSON-syntax flag in this doc at once. Do this before writing a single line of datapack. |
| **2** | 🔬 **MEASURE** | **Time the Cataclysm gauntlet with 6 players.** The entire band is derived from `H=40`. If it's really 25, every verdict below shifts. | 1 session | ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ | You are balancing against a number you guessed. Fix that first or everything downstream is fiction. |
| **3** | ⬇️ **NERF** | **Iron's Spellbooks — install Ars 'n Spells, set `spell_power_cap = 1.40`** (§2.1) | 30 min | ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ | 5.1x anchor, *same axis* as Ars Nouveau, and the reward is a **craftable recipe** so R5 multiplies it by 6. Worst single defect in the pack, and the fix is one config line in a mod that already solved it. |
| **4** | ⬆️ **DE-GATE** | **Audit every armor/weapon recipe for `create:` ingredients; add non-Create alternatives** (§2.4) | 1–2 hr | ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ | R=0.0009 is 12x under floor — the largest deviation in the table **in either direction**. Also the #1 complaint about Beyond Depth. Cheap to prevent, miserable to retrofit. |
| **5** | ⬇️ **NERF** | **Simply Swords — move uniques from structure loot to boss drops** (§2.2, §3.5) | 1 hr | ⭐⭐⭐⭐ | R=0.0625, the highest rate in the pack, and it's high because `H=8`, not because P is huge. Fixing H is a datapack edit; fixing P would need scripting. Take the cheap win. |
| **6** | 🧱 **INFRA** | **Install Lootr** (§1.5.2) | 10 min | ⭐⭐⭐⭐ | Per-player chests. Kills the first-to-the-chest race *and* the R5 gifting divisor on all loot tracks simultaneously. Ten minutes, fixes a category. |
| **7** | 🧱 **INFRA** | **Install KubeJS + LootJS + Paxi + spark + Chunky** (§2.9) | 30 min | ⭐⭐⭐⭐ | These are the tools every fix below needs. Retrofitting them mid-campaign is how balance work stops happening. |
| **8** | ⬇️ **NERF** | **Apotheosis — cut the top affix rarity tier, halve damage affix magnitudes** (§2.3) | 2 hr | ⭐⭐⭐⭐ | 3.8x anchor with an **unbounded tail**. Six farmers will find the tail. ⚠ Test the Architectury conflict first — if it crashes, this becomes a mod-selection call instead. |
| **9** | ⬆️ **BUFF** | **Give the Structure Explorer track a named capstone item** (§1.5.1) | 1 hr | ⭐⭐⭐ | R=0.0056, floor-adjacent, and its rewards are *diffuse*. Humans compare trophies. One nameable item fixes the perception without raising the ceiling. |
| **10** | ⬆️ **BUFF** | **Rewrite vanilla Wither / Dragon loot tables to pay competitively** (§2.10) | 1 hr | ⭐⭐⭐ | Free datapack lever nobody uses. Raises the floor so modded rewards don't feel mandatory. |
| **11** | ⬇️ **NERF** | **Sharpness → `max_level: 4`** (§3.6) | 15 min | ⭐⭐⭐ | Lowers the *entire* curve with one file. Every "is this too strong" question gets easier. Do it early — changing the baseline later invalidates all measurements. |
| **12** | 🔬 **MEASURE** | **Antarchy — run the §2.8 measurement procedure** | 30 min | ⭐⭐⭐ | The owner named it as the offender and I have **zero** data. Could be the worst thing in the pack or already fine. Unknown = unactionable. |
| **13** | ⬆️ **BUFF** | **Shorten Undergarden, or accept the 1.5x gap vs Deeper and Darker** (§2.7) | 1 hr | ⭐⭐ | Both in band, but 1.5x on the same axis is at the edge of noticeable. Low priority; revisit if anyone complains. |
| **14** | 🧱 **INFRA** | **AttributeFix global clamp: `attack_damage max 20.0`** (§2.3) | 20 min | ⭐⭐ | Safety net, not a fix. Install **after** the targeted work so you can tell what the net actually caught. Blunt — will clip things you didn't intend. |
| **15** | 📣 **COMMS** | **Post the §4 track menu to Discord before launch** | 10 min | ⭐⭐⭐⭐ | Prevents the argument rather than settling it. Arguably belongs at #1; it's here only because the menu's numbers depend on fixes 1–5 landing first. |

**If you only do five things: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6.** Two measurements, one nerf, one de-gate, one
infra mod. That covers both extremes of the table and takes under three hours.

