# 6. TESTING PROTOCOL

## 6.1 Measuring a track's real P and H (do this before fixing anything)

### Measuring P — the exact commands

```
/gamemode creative
/give @s <mod:the_reward_item>
```

Hold it, then:

```
# Your total attack damage including the held item's modifier.
# NOTE: 1.21 dropped the `generic.` prefix. It is `minecraft:attack_damage`, NOT
# `minecraft:generic.attack_damage`. The old form silently fails.
/attribute @s minecraft:attack_damage get

/attribute @s minecraft:attack_speed get
/attribute @s minecraft:armor get
/attribute @s minecraft:armor_toughness get
/attribute @s minecraft:max_health get
```

Ground truth for the item itself:

```
/data get entity @s SelectedItem
```

That prints the full component map including `minecraft:attribute_modifiers` with real amounts
and operations. **This is the number that matters** — the `/attribute get` value can be polluted
by other equipment.

Then:

```
DPS = attack_damage × attack_speed
P   = DPS / 18.0
```

### Measuring P for non-damage rewards

There's no clean command. Use a comparison harness:

1. Build a flat test arena. Summon a fixed target:
   `/summon minecraft:zombie ~ ~ ~5 {Attributes:[{id:"minecraft:max_health",base:200}],Health:200f,NoAI:1b}`
   ⚠ **VERIFY** the `Attributes` NBT key shape on 1.21.1 — attribute NBT changed around 1.21 and
   I'm not certain of the exact form. If it errors, summon a normal mob and use
   `/attribute @e[type=zombie,limit=1] minecraft:max_health base set 200`.
2. Time how long it takes to kill with (a) Sharp V netherite, (b) the track's reward. Ratio = P.
3. For mobility/utility rewards, P isn't measurable this way — **fall back to R4 (axis match)
   and just make sure it's honestly advertised in the menu.** Don't fake a number.

### Measuring H

**There is no shortcut. Play it, or ask someone who has.** The dishonest move is estimating H
optimistically because you already know the route. Estimate H for *a player who doesn't know
where anything is*, because that's who's playing.

Cheapest real method: have each of the six log their track's hours in a Discord thread. After
two weeks you have real H values and the whole table becomes fact instead of guesswork.

## 6.2 Verifying a nerf actually landed

**Config changes:**
1. Stop the server fully. **Edit configs only while stopped** — many mods rewrite their config
   from memory on shutdown and will clobber a live edit. (Same trap as Palworld's
   `PalWorldSettings.ini`; see the project CLAUDE.md.)
2. Restart.
3. `/reload` does **not** reload mod TOML configs. Only a restart does.
4. Re-run the §6.1 measurement. **Compare numbers, not vibes.**

**Datapack changes:**
1. `/reload` — this *does* reload datapacks.
2. Watch the console. A malformed JSON file produces a **red parse error naming the file**. If
   you see no error and no change, your **path is wrong** — the file loaded as a new,
   never-referenced resource. This is the #1 failure mode and it looks identical to "nothing
   happened."
3. `/datapack list` — confirm `bd-balance` is in the **enabled** list, not available-but-disabled.

**Recipe changes:** open **JEI**, search the item, press **U** (uses) and **R** (recipes).
JEI reads the *live* recipe manager, so if JEI still shows the old recipe after `/reload`, the
override didn't land. **JEI is your fastest recipe-verification tool by a wide margin.**

**Loot changes:**
```
/loot give @s loot <namespace>:<path>
```
Runs the table straight into your inventory. If the path is wrong you get an immediate error,
which is exactly the feedback you want. Run it 20 times to eyeball the distribution.

**Enchantment changes:**
```
/enchant @s minecraft:sharpness 5
```
If you set `max_level: 4`, this should now **fail**. If it succeeds, your enchantment JSON
didn't load — go back to the `/reload` console output.

## 6.3 The parity test bench

Make a permanent creative test world (Paxi means your balance datapack loads into it
automatically). Keep a function at
`data/bdbalance/function/debug/parity_kit.mcfunction` that gives you the baseline in one command:

```mcfunction
# The P=1.00 reference loadout. Everything gets measured against this.
give @s minecraft:netherite_sword[minecraft:enchantments={levels:{"minecraft:sharpness":5}}] 1
give @s minecraft:netherite_helmet[minecraft:enchantments={levels:{"minecraft:protection":4}}] 1
give @s minecraft:netherite_chestplate[minecraft:enchantments={levels:{"minecraft:protection":4}}] 1
give @s minecraft:netherite_leggings[minecraft:enchantments={levels:{"minecraft:protection":4}}] 1
give @s minecraft:netherite_boots[minecraft:enchantments={levels:{"minecraft:protection":4}}] 1
```

⚠ **VERIFY the component syntax.** 1.21 replaced NBT with components and the enchantment
component shape (`minecraft:enchantments={levels:{...}}`) changed again in 1.21.5. Test the
first line by hand before saving the file.

Run it, measure, write the numbers in the Section 1.3 table. **Then** measure the mod. Never
measure a mod without re-measuring the baseline in the same session — it takes 10 seconds and it
catches the case where a previous change moved the floor.

## 6.4 Bisecting a crash in a 150-mod pack

**Before you bisect, read the crash report.** Most of the time you don't need to bisect at all.

```powershell
# Newest crash report
Get-ChildItem "C:\Game Servers\Minecraft\server\crash-reports" |
  Sort-Object LastWriteTime -Descending | Select-Object -First 1
```

In the stack trace, the **first non-`minecraft`/non-`neoforge` package name** is usually your
culprit. NeoForge also prints a mod list at the bottom of the report — cross-reference.

If the game **won't start** and the log is unhelpful, bisect:

```powershell
$mods = "C:\Game Servers\Minecraft\server\mods"
$park = "C:\Game Servers\Minecraft\server\mods-parked"
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force $park

# Move half the jars out
$all  = Get-ChildItem $mods -Filter *.jar | Sort-Object Name
$half = $all | Select-Object -First ([math]::Floor($all.Count / 2))
$half | Move-Item -Destination $park
```

Start. Crash persists → the culprit is in the remaining half. Crash gone → it's in the parked
half. Move half of the guilty set back and repeat. **150 mods → ~8 restarts.**

**Three things that make bisection actually work:**
1. **Never move library mods.** Keep JEI, Architectury, Curios, GeckoLib, Balm, Moonlight,
   YUNG's API, Puzzles Lib, Kotlin for Forge, Bookshelf, Collective, Resourceful Lib,
   Cloth Config, TerraBlender in `mods/` for every round. Moving a library produces a
   *different* crash and poisons the bisect. Park them in a separate `libs/` folder mentally
   and treat them as untouchable.
2. **Bisect on a scratch world**, never the real one. A half-modded world load will strip
   entities and blocks belonging to the parked mods, permanently.
3. **Note the crash's exception class each round.** If the exception changes, you didn't
   isolate the original bug — you found a second one. Back up a step.

**Known conflicts to check before bisecting** (these are documented and version-specific):
- **`java.lang.IllegalStateException: Mod 'architectury' is not available!`** with Architectury
  clearly installed → a mod is bundling **OmegaConfig**, which prevents Architectury from
  initializing. Hunt the bundled OmegaConfig; don't reinstall Architectury.
  ([report](https://github.com/lostmyself8/Mekanism-Extras-neoforge/issues/20))
- **`Conflicting default methods: ...DeferredSupplier.getKey`** → the Architectury/Apotheosis
  conflict, [issue #592](https://github.com/architectury/architectury-api/issues/592), still open.
- **Cloth Config version mismatch** → Cloth routes through Architectury on NeoForge. **Pin one
  Architectury build at pack-freeze and never let the launcher auto-bump it mid-campaign.**

## 6.5 Performance verification (32GB box, six players, also a gaming rig)

**Profile before you tune.** Guessing at RAM on this machine is a known failure mode (Palworld's
crashes traced to the RAM ceiling; see CLAUDE.md).

```
/spark profiler start --timeout 300
/spark heapsummary
/spark tps
```

On a 150-mod pack the bottleneck is almost always either **(a)** one badly-behaved mod's tick
handler or **(b)** heap pressure from mod registries. ModernFix and FerriteCore address (b).
**Nothing addresses (a) except finding it**, and spark is the only way to find it.

**Chunky-pregenerate the spawn region before launch night.** It removes the single largest source
of "why is the server lagging" complaints in week one, and it costs you an afternoon of unattended
CPU time instead of six people's evening.

